= main =

== foundations of arithmetic ==
    In the enquiry that follows, I have kept to three fundamental principles:
        * Always to separate sharply the psychological from the logical, the
          subjective from the objective;
        * never to ask for the meaning of a word in isolation, but only in the
          context of a proposition;
        * never to lose sight of the distinction between concept and object.
			[[local:zz.texts/frege1884-foundationsOfArithmetic.austinTrans.pdf]]

A (the?) key move is Frege's claim that for each equivalence relationship there
is something (an object in the domain of quantification) that is what is common
to the objects in that relationship to each other.

equivalence relation (reflexive, symmetric and transitive)

Examples: same length/pitch/mass, parallel, 


aRb <=> x⃰(aRx) = x⃰(xRb)

Now, precisely because R is supposed to be an
Look at this as a way of eliminating x⃰

Frege's method is to define the number 5 as what is common between all
collections that are equinumerous with (say) the fingers on my left hand.


=== objection ===
==== is Julius Caesar the number 5? ====

Frege's method of defining number as 'what is common between all objects that
are related by an equivalence relation' (such as having a 1-1 mapping between
them can define what the numbers are) doesn't settle the truth value of the
claim “Julius Caesar is the number 5”.



